---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const overrideSnippet = `@import "@kernelui-lib/styles";

:root {
  --kernel-hue-accent: 260;      /* → every accent shade re-hues */
  --kernel-radius-base: 1.25rem; /* → every corner re-rounds */
  --kernel-space-unit: 0.25rem;  /* → every gap and padding rescales */
}`;

const layerSnippet = `@import "@kernelui-lib/styles";
@import "@kernelui-lib/react/styles.layer.css";
/* or @kernelui-lib/elements/styles.layer.css */`;

const layerOverrideSnippet = `/* Unlayered, so it beats Kernel's rules at any specificity. */
.checkout-button {
  border-radius: 0;
  text-transform: uppercase;
}`;

const layerResetSnippet = `/* Layer order is set by first mention, so this goes above the imports. */
@layer reset, kernel.tokens, kernel.base, kernel.components;
@import "@kernelui-lib/styles";
@import "@kernelui-lib/react/styles.layer.css";

@layer reset {
  button { background: none; border: 0; }
}`;

const presetSnippet =`<html data-kernel-accent="violet">`;

const scaleSnippet = `--kernel-accent-1   through -2   backgrounds
--kernel-accent-3   through -5   component background (rest / hover / pressed)
--kernel-accent-6   through -8   borders (static / interactive / hover)
--kernel-accent-9   through -10  solid fill (normal / hover)
--kernel-accent-11  through -12  text (low-contrast / high-contrast)`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Theming" description="Every CSS custom property that drives Kernel's colours, spacing, radius, and typography — plus named accent presets." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Theming</h1>
    <p>
      Every colour, radius, spacing, and duration a component uses comes
      from a CSS custom property in <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code> — there's
      no per-component theme prop, no config file. Override a token on{" "}
      <code>:root</code> (or on any container, for scoped theming) and it
      reaches every component reading it, immediately, including whichever
      accent and radius you've picked from the theme menu in this site's own
      header.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={overrideSnippet} lang="css" />

    <h2>Colour scale</h2>
    <p>
      Colours follow Radix Colors' scale model: a 12-step ramp per role
      (accent, gray, danger, success, warning), generated in OKLCH from one
      hue and one chroma rather than 12 hand-picked swatches. Every step has
      one fixed job, so choosing a colour is choosing a step, not guessing a
      shade:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={scaleSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>
      Components never read a raw scale step directly — they read semantic
      aliases (<code>--kernel-color-accent</code>,{" "}
      <code>--kernel-color-border</code>, <code>--kernel-color-text-muted</code>,
      and similar) that point at a specific step. Change{" "}
      <code>--kernel-hue-accent</code> and every alias downstream re-hues at
      once.
    </p>

    <h2>Accent presets</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--kernel-hue-accent</code> reshades the accent scale with one
      shared lightness/chroma curve across every hue — close, but not
      individually calibrated per hue. For a curated, gamut-checked
      alternative, seven named presets (amber, blue, green, red, violet,
      orange, teal) ship as importable stylesheets, each hand-checked for
      the punchiest chroma that still renders in sRGB at every step:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={presetSnippet} lang="html" />
    <p>
      This is exactly what the theme menu in the header writes when you pick
      an accent swatch — a <code>data-kernel-accent</code> attribute on{" "}
      <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, persisted to <code>localStorage</code> so it
      survives navigation.
    </p>

    <h2>Contrast</h2>
    <p>
      Kernel measures colour contrast with
      <a href="https://github.com/Myndex/SAPC-APCA">APCA</a>, the
      Accessible Perceptual Contrast Algorithm proposed for WCAG 3. It keeps
      the WCAG 2 contrast ratio as a floor, because that's still the standard
      most accessibility requirements cite.
    </p>
    <p>
      APCA models how light and dark colours are actually perceived. That
      matters most in dark mode, where WCAG 2's ratio overrates contrast:
      Kernel's old focus ring passed WCAG 2 at about 4.2:1 in dark mode but
      scored only Lc 30 under APCA, and it was visibly faint.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>Contrast targets for Kernel's semantic tokens, on the canvas and surface.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Token</th><th scope="col">Used for</th><th scope="col">APCA</th><th scope="col">WCAG 2 floor</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--kernel-color-text</code></td><td>Body text</td><td>Lc 75</td><td>4.5:1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--kernel-color-text-muted</code></td><td>Secondary text</td><td>Lc 60</td><td>4.5:1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--kernel-color-focus-ring</code></td><td>Focus outlines</td><td>Lc 45</td><td>3:1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--kernel-color-border-control</code></td><td>Checkbox, radio and switch edges</td><td>Lc 45</td><td>3:1</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      The text levels are APCA's own minimums for body text and for other
      content text. Focus rings and control borders are 1–2px lines, which
      APCA treats as fine detail with a higher minimum than solid shapes, so
      Kernel holds them to Lc 45, its level for fine-detail icons.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every accent preset is checked in light and dark mode on every change
      (<code>bun run test:contrast</code>). If you override these tokens,
      check your values with an APCA calculator such as
      <a href="https://apcacontrast.com">apcacontrast.com</a> against the
      same targets. APCA is still a draft and may change.
    </p>

    <h2>Shape</h2>
    <p>
      One radius token, <code>--kernel-radius-base</code>, drives every
      corner on the site. Components read a semantic tier —{" "}
      <code>--kernel-radius-control</code> for standalone controls (buttons,
      inputs), <code>--kernel-radius-container</code> and{" "}
      <code>--kernel-radius-sheet</code> for surfaces that wrap them — and
      those container tiers are generated as{" "}
      <code>--kernel-radius-base + that container's own padding</code>, so a
      button nested inside a card shares a concentric corner with it instead
      of just looking independently rounded.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because those tiers are derived from padding, the radius grows with{" "}
      <code>--kernel-radius-base</code> while a flat padding value doesn't —
      so a surface that pads with a raw space token looks right at the
      default and reads as text crammed into a big curve once you turn
      rounding up. Each container tier therefore has a padding token to pair
      with: <code>--kernel-padding-container-curve</code> for{" "}
      <code>--kernel-radius-container</code>, and{" "}
      <code>--kernel-padding-sheet-curve</code> for{" "}
      <code>--kernel-radius-sheet</code>. Both are the flat padding plus the
      same proportional term the radius already has — nothing extra at
      Sharp, +50% at Round. Use them for anything you build on top of these
      tokens, and switch the rounding control above to Round to check it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Controls share one more layer of DNA. Every standalone control sits on
      the same height scale — <code>--kernel-control-height-sm</code>,{" "}
      <code>-md</code> and <code>-lg</code> — so a button, a text field and a
      select line up in a row without matching numbers by hand. A control
      that holds pieces of its own, like the selected segment in Tabs or the
      fill of an inset Slider, frames them with{" "}
      <code>--kernel-control-inset</code> and rounds them with{" "}
      <code>--kernel-radius-control-inner</code>: the control radius minus
      that inset, so the inner and outer corners stay concentric at Sharp,
      Soft and Round alike.
    </p>

    <h2>Space</h2>
    <p>
      Every gap and padding is a multiple of one unit,{" "}
      <code>--kernel-space-unit</code> (<code>--kernel-space-1</code>{" "}
      through <code>--kernel-space-12</code>). Changing the unit rescales
      the entire site's density at once, rather than each component picking
      its own gap value.
    </p>

    <h2>Elevation</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--kernel-shadow-sm/md/lg</code> are baked multi-layer shadows,
      not single blurred offsets or a runtime plugin. Each tier combines a
      crisp contact edge, a short umbra, and a wider low-opacity ambient
      layer for smoother falloff. The same tokens adapt their edge and
      falloff contrast through <code>light-dark()</code>, so cards,
      popovers, menus, and dialogs keep their depth in either theme.
    </p>

    <h2>Type and motion</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--kernel-font-sans</code> and <code>--kernel-font-mono</code>{" "}
      use system font stacks by default. The package includes no webfonts;
      load your own fonts and override these tokens to customise typography.{" "}
      <code>--kernel-duration-fast/base/slow</code> and{" "}
      <code>--kernel-ease-spring/out/in</code> drive every transition the
      same way: change the curve once, every hover, open, and close
      animation on the page moves the same way.{" "}
      <code>--kernel-scale-press</code> is press feedback for anything
      clickable, <code>--kernel-blur-xs/sm/md/backdrop</code> is motion
      blur for entering/exiting content and glass surfaces, and{" "}
      <code>--kernel-ease-overshoot</code> is a deliberate exception —
      the only token that overshoots past 1 — reserved for a one-shot
      size or shape change like a morphing container, never for
      reversible overlay motion.
    </p>

    <h2 id="overriding-component-styles">Overriding component styles</h2>
    <p>
      Tokens cover most changes. When you need to restyle a component
      itself, import the layered stylesheet in place of the plain one:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={layerSnippet} lang="css" />
    <p>
      <code>styles.layer.css</code> holds the same rules as{" "}
      <code>styles.css</code>, inside <code>@layer kernel.components</code>,
      and declares the order <code>kernel.tokens</code>,{" "}
      <code>kernel.base</code>, <code>kernel.components</code>. The tokens
      and reset in <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code> are already layered.
      Any CSS you write outside a layer then beats every Kernel rule,
      whatever its specificity and wherever it loads, so a plain class
      selector is enough and you never need <code>!important</code>. Target
      a component through your own <code>className</code>, its{" "}
      <code>data-*</code> state attributes, or its stable{" "}
      <code>kernel-Name-part</code> class names.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={layerOverrideSnippet} lang="css" />
    <p>
      The plain <code>styles.css</code> stays unlayered on purpose. Layered
      rules lose to every unlayered rule, including a CSS reset's bare
      element selectors (<code>button {"{"} background: none {"}"}</code>),
      so switching an existing app over silently could let its reset strip
      Kernel's styling. If your own CSS includes a broad reset, put it in a
      layer named before Kernel's, or keep using <code>styles.css</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={layerResetSnippet} lang="css" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
